<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nagapattinam</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Negapatnam, Negapatam</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nagapattinam is a port town on the coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamil-nadu">Tamil Nadu</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, south of the Kaveri delta. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chola-empire">Cholas</a> it was a major harbour with a Buddhist monastery built with the support of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/srivijaya">Srivijaya</a> kings. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> took it from the Portuguese in 1658 and made it the capital of their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coromandel-coast">Coromandel</a> possessions from 1690, until the British captured it in 1781.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nagapattinam.jpg" alt="Nagapattinam" width="320" height="400" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Aspandi007 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nagapattinam_Documentary.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Nagapattinam lies on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bay-of-bengal">Bay of Bengal</a>, on a plain of alluvial soil of sand, silt and clay, bounded on the south by the Uppanar river. It is about 300 kilometres from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chennai">Chennai</a>. The Vettar River and tributaries of the Kaveri are the main water bodies, and paddy is the chief crop of the region. The climate is tropical savanna, with a wet season from August to December brought by the northeast <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monsoon">monsoon</a>, and the town is in a cyclone-prone zone.</p><p>The name comes from Nagar, a reference to people from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a> who settled here, and pattinam, 'town'. Ptolemy mentions it as Nikam, one of the most important trade centres of the ancient Tamil country, and the poets <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/appar">Appar</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sambandar">Sambandar</a> call it Nagai in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tevaram">Tevaram</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Urn burials from the Sangam period show early habitation. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tevaram">Tevaram</a> poets of the 7th century describe a town with fortified walls and a busy port, and inscriptions suggest that the Kayarohanaswami Temple was begun under the Pallava king Narasimha Pallava II (691–729 CE). The town came to prominence under the Medieval Cholas and was their important port and a gateway for eastern naval expeditions; all those of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajendra-chola-i">Rajendra Chola I</a> (1012–44) went through it. In the 11th century the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sailendra-dynasty">Sailendra</a> king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/srivijaya">Srivijaya</a>, Sri Mara Vijayattungavarman, built the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chudamani-vihara">Chudamani Vihara</a>, a Buddhist monastery, with the patronage of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajaraja-i">Rajaraja Chola I</a>.</p><p>The Portuguese made commercial contacts in the early 16th century and set up a commercial centre in 1554, and also carried on missionary work. Under an agreement with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nayaka">Nayak</a> king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thanjavur">Thanjavur</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> received ten villages from the Portuguese, built ten churches and a hospital, and issued pagoda coins with the town's name in Tamil. Nagapattinam became the capital of Dutch Coromandel.</p><p>After naval battles between British and French fleets off the coast, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a> took it from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> in 1781, and a peace in 1784 formally ceded it. It was the headquarters of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thanjavur">Tanjore</a> district from 1799 to 1845 and became a municipality in 1866. The Great Southern of India Railway had its headquarters there from 1861 to 1875.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Nagapattinam is a city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamil-nadu">Tamil Nadu</a> and the headquarters of Nagapattinam district, created in 1991. It had 102,905 inhabitants in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> of 2011. Fishing in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bay-of-bengal">Bay of Bengal</a> is the main occupation, alongside trade and tourism. The Soundararajaperumal Temple, a Divya Desam of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vishnu">Vishnu</a>, and the Kayarohanaswami Temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a> are major pilgrimage sites, and the town is a base for visiting Nagore and Velankanni. The tsunami of 26 December 2004 struck the district severely.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Nagapattinam” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Chola country lay in the flat plain of the Kaveri and its tributaries, now the districts of Thanjavur, Tiruchirapalli, Tiruvarur, <b>Nagapattinam</b> and others.</blockquote><a href="/empire/chola-empire">Chola Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Srivijayan rulers also endowed Buddhist foundations abroad, including a monastery at Nalanda in Bihar and the Chudamani Vihara at <b>Nagapattinam</b> on the Chola coast.</blockquote><a href="/empire/srivijaya-empire">Srivijaya Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Further factories followed at <b>Negapatnam</b>, Sadras and Bimlipatnam, forming the governorate of Coromandel.</blockquote><a href="/state/dutch-coromandel">Dutch Coromandel</a></li></ul></section>
